# tcltest ships with Tcl, so there is nothing to install. `package require`
# loads it and `namespace import` brings its commands into scope unqualified,
# which is what lets `test` below be written bare.
package require tcltest
namespace import ::tcltest::*

# Sourcing the code under test. The path is built rather than written: `info
# script` is this file, `file dirname` is its directory, and `file join` puts the
# pieces together with the right separator -- so the suite runs from anywhere.
source [file join [file dirname [info script]] .. src {{PROJECT_SNAKE}}.tcl]

# Nothing names this file. `make test` runs every tests/*.test, so a test file is
# found by that extension and deleting one is safe. Run them with `make test`.
#
# `test` takes a name, a description, and then options. The first argument is an
# identifier you would see in a summary line and the second is the sentence a
# reader wants -- so the pair is `greet-1` plus the description, and the
# description is what to write carefully.
#
# -body is the code to run and -result is what it should produce. The comparison
# is exact string equality by default, and -match can make it a glob or a
# regexp instead.
#
# Both are in braces rather than quotes, which matters: braces pass the text
# through unsubstituted, so the body is evaluated when the test runs rather than
# interpolated now. A hash inside a braced body is not a comment.
test greet-1 {a greeting addresses the name it is given} -body {
    {{PROJECT_SNAKE}}::greet world
} -result {Hello, world!}

# Prints the summary and sets the exit status. Without it a failing test reports
# itself and the process still exits zero.
cleanupTests
